{"type":"meta","version":"0.1.1","title":"The Solar System","created":"2026-04-27T12:00:00+00:00","lastModified":"2026-04-27T12:00:00+00:00"}
{"type":"extension","id":"semantic-anchors","data":[{"word":"Heliosphere","description":"The vast bubble of solar wind and magnetic field that surrounds the Sun, extending well past Neptune's orbit.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Astronomical Unit","description":"The mean Earth-Sun distance, ~149.6 million km — the standard yardstick for distances within the solar system.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Protoplanetary Disk","description":"The flattened, rotating disk of gas and dust around a young star from which planets accrete.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Late Heavy Bombardment","description":"A roughly 4.1–3.8 Gya episode of intense impacts on the inner solar system, recorded in Moon and Mercury cratering.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Terrestrial Planet","description":"A rocky inner planet — Mercury, Venus, Earth, or Mars — composed mainly of silicate rock and metal.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Gas Giant","description":"A massive outer planet (Jupiter or Saturn) dominated by a thick hydrogen-helium envelope around a dense core.","knowMoreUrl":null},{"word":"Roche Limit","description":"The minimum distance at which a satellite held together only by gravity can orbit a primary without being tidally torn apart.","knowMoreUrl":null}]}
{"type":"layer","id":"04c8745b-46e7-461c-ac9f-2faa64b16335","level":0,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"0416aed1-63e3-4fe3-804a-077a3ea41bbe","position":{"x":0,"y":0},"data":{"label":"The Solar System","summary":"Our star, the Sun, plus eight planets, hundreds of moons, and countless small bodies bound by gravity — formed ~4.6 billion years ago from a collapsing molecular cloud.","color":"#fff699"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[],"parentLayerId":null,"parentNodeId":null}
{"type":"layer","id":"83326a2b-3774-4957-8878-e1280bab010f","level":1,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"a33a3e1e-6ce1-4329-acb5-ebf8fa554bd6","position":{"x":0,"y":-320},"data":{"label":"The Sun","summary":"A G-type main-sequence star, 1.39 million km across, 4.6 Gyr old, fusing 600 million tonnes of hydrogen per second in its core.","color":"#ffcfa1"}},{"id":"eb45451e-8a62-482e-9817-558674d78928","position":{"x":304,"y":-99},"data":{"label":"Inner Planets","summary":"Four rocky worlds — Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars — packed inside 1.5 AU. Small, dense, slow-rotating with thin or no rings.","color":"#faada5"}},{"id":"3bb6090b-9ba8-40ef-88c1-74efcbd1ce2a","position":{"x":188,"y":259},"data":{"label":"Outer Planets","summary":"Four giants — Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune — between 5 and 30 AU. Massive, ringed, hydrogen/helium or icy compositions.","color":"#b7e7f3"}},{"id":"6aca519f-31d2-4ab8-965d-984453599947","position":{"x":-188,"y":259},"data":{"label":"Small Bodies","summary":"Asteroids (~1M+ in the main belt), comets, Kuiper Belt objects, and the distant Oort Cloud — leftover planet-building material.","color":"#bcaea1"}},{"id":"da351a86-2756-49f4-aff2-7625f7fc0d73","position":{"x":-304,"y":-99},"data":{"label":"Formation History","summary":"Solar nebula collapsed ~4.6 Gya into a protoplanetary disk; planetesimals accreted, gas giants migrated, and the Late Heavy Bombardment shaped early surfaces.","color":"#d5bcfe"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[],"parentLayerId":"04c8745b-46e7-461c-ac9f-2faa64b16335","parentNodeId":"0416aed1-63e3-4fe3-804a-077a3ea41bbe"}
{"type":"layer","id":"54488d53-0d39-4bae-87f3-1ec6f94117ca","level":2,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"6a073788-0926-421d-a668-3e92765d1dae","position":{"x":0,"y":-260},"data":{"label":"Core","summary":"Innermost 25 % by radius; 15 million °C, 250 g/cm³. Site of hydrogen-to-helium fusion — produces all the Sun's energy.","color":"#ffcfa1"}},{"id":"4ec8ce9b-678b-4d7f-af35-d673bcc17604","position":{"x":260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Radiative Zone","summary":"From 0.25 to 0.7 solar radii. Photons random-walk outward, taking ~170 000 years to traverse this dense plasma.","color":"#ffcfa1"}},{"id":"a4bd9a21-8078-4672-a288-24b82350ed1f","position":{"x":0,"y":260},"data":{"label":"Convective Zone","summary":"Outer 30 %. Plasma cells rise, cool, and sink in 11-day overturn cells, transporting heat by bulk motion.","color":"#ffcfa1"}},{"id":"11e0e5c0-a628-4f6d-a425-790cea34c6f9","position":{"x":-260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Photosphere","summary":"Visible surface, ~5500 °C. Granulation pattern (1000 km cells) marks the tops of convection columns.","color":"#ffcfa1"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[{"id":"566b524b-6f42-4f30-91e4-afa2f4089801","nodes":["6a073788-0926-421d-a668-3e92765d1dae","4ec8ce9b-678b-4d7f-af35-d673bcc17604"],"data":{"label":"Energy core","formation":"radial","style":"dashed"}}],"parentLayerId":"83326a2b-3774-4957-8878-e1280bab010f","parentNodeId":"a33a3e1e-6ce1-4329-acb5-ebf8fa554bd6"}
{"type":"layer","id":"52452a6c-85fb-479a-adef-ac83b498c4ec","level":2,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"ce1d5269-bac8-4595-8a9e-48df1ad76322","position":{"x":0,"y":-260},"data":{"label":"Mercury","summary":"0.39 AU from the Sun; 4880 km diameter; no atmosphere; surface temp swings 430 °C (day) to −180 °C (night).","color":"#faada5"}},{"id":"c754c1a0-d350-4efc-a463-5e3acfd0e806","position":{"x":260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Venus","summary":"0.72 AU; 12 100 km; thick CO₂ atmosphere at 92 bar; runaway greenhouse keeps surface at 465 °C.","color":"#faada5"}},{"id":"f7e89a19-3437-4911-ae52-9cdbae72b8df","position":{"x":0,"y":260},"data":{"label":"Earth","summary":"1.00 AU; 12 742 km; nitrogen-oxygen atmosphere; liquid water; one large moon; only known biosphere.","color":"#faada5"}},{"id":"efd9f66a-90f0-487a-ad2d-4763c8e172df","position":{"x":-260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Mars","summary":"1.52 AU; 6779 km; thin CO₂ atmosphere; polar ice caps; Olympus Mons (22 km tall) and Valles Marineris (4000 km long).","color":"#faada5"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[{"id":"86bbe7bf-1d87-4aeb-8da9-43e2c41d47ba","nodes":["ce1d5269-bac8-4595-8a9e-48df1ad76322","c754c1a0-d350-4efc-a463-5e3acfd0e806","f7e89a19-3437-4911-ae52-9cdbae72b8df","efd9f66a-90f0-487a-ad2d-4763c8e172df"],"data":{"label":"Terrestrial worlds","formation":"radial","style":"dashed"}}],"parentLayerId":"83326a2b-3774-4957-8878-e1280bab010f","parentNodeId":"eb45451e-8a62-482e-9817-558674d78928"}
{"type":"layer","id":"5995a5ac-95b9-4366-a6f3-4ad757618139","level":2,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"bca1da80-1152-4410-9629-3041f9c4ee65","position":{"x":0,"y":-260},"data":{"label":"Jupiter","summary":"5.2 AU; 139 820 km — 11× Earth radius; 318 Earth masses; 95 known moons; Great Red Spot storm 16 000 km wide.","color":"#b7e7f3"}},{"id":"7bcd0e8a-f7d7-4116-badf-0a0ea7cc0a40","position":{"x":260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Saturn","summary":"9.5 AU; 116 460 km; spectacular ring system (main rings span 7000–80 000 km from cloud-tops); 146 moons.","color":"#b7e7f3"}},{"id":"34605651-28cb-40fd-8ba4-7d257b85cc3d","position":{"x":0,"y":260},"data":{"label":"Uranus","summary":"19.2 AU; 50 724 km; tilted 98° on its axis; methane gives its pale-blue color; faint dark rings.","color":"#b7e7f3"}},{"id":"88b805c9-d712-4c90-a37e-ae56c6d13791","position":{"x":-260,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Neptune","summary":"30.1 AU; 49 244 km; supersonic winds up to 2100 km/h — the fastest in the solar system; deep-blue methane atmosphere.","color":"#b7e7f3"}}],"connections":[],"containers":[{"id":"7d8854c1-c057-44c2-8ca4-f323ca7d7723","nodes":["bca1da80-1152-4410-9629-3041f9c4ee65","7bcd0e8a-f7d7-4116-badf-0a0ea7cc0a40"],"data":{"label":"Gas giants","formation":"radial","style":"dashed"}},{"id":"c5d20e10-9118-4cf1-9d55-a4a0903c65b4","nodes":["34605651-28cb-40fd-8ba4-7d257b85cc3d","88b805c9-d712-4c90-a37e-ae56c6d13791"],"data":{"label":"Ice giants","formation":"radial","style":"none"}}],"parentLayerId":"83326a2b-3774-4957-8878-e1280bab010f","parentNodeId":"3bb6090b-9ba8-40ef-88c1-74efcbd1ce2a"}
{"type":"layer","id":"24e2c8f9-1cd7-477d-bedc-9c7ae9fd0511","level":2,"layout":"linear-horizontal","nodes":[{"id":"7bcec2f8-3e62-4c54-ade0-770a4f7680aa","position":{"x":-750,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Solar Nebula 4.6 Gya","summary":"Giant molecular cloud collapses under gravity; conservation of angular momentum flattens it into a spinning disk.","color":"#d5bcfe"}},{"id":"463b7b16-3123-49da-939b-466a6e6e9d02","position":{"x":-500,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Protoplanetary Disk","summary":"Hot inner disk vaporizes ices; cold outer disk retains them, setting up the rocky/gas divide at the frost line (~4 AU).","color":"#d5bcfe"}},{"id":"d85476d4-de2f-4304-87cb-b626d95c55e3","position":{"x":-250,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Planetesimals Accrete","summary":"Dust grains stick into pebbles, then km-scale planetesimals, then runaway gravitational accretion forms protoplanets.","color":"#d5bcfe"}},{"id":"dae92517-52ff-442d-bfb9-817b427ebb96","position":{"x":0,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Inner Planets Form","summary":"Mercury through Mars complete primary accretion within ~10 Myr; differentiation separates iron cores from silicate mantles.","color":"#d5bcfe"}},{"id":"0a2aa224-8d3c-4cfb-8a5f-1dd3f2904783","position":{"x":250,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Late Heavy Bombardment","summary":"~4.1–3.8 Gya: gas-giant migration scatters small bodies inward; intense impacts cratering the Moon, Mercury, and early Earth.","color":"#d5bcfe"}},{"id":"9da0937b-342d-4ff6-a5a0-a53d46efec2d","position":{"x":500,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Planetary Migration","summary":"Jupiter and Saturn cross resonances; Neptune and Uranus settle into final orbits; Kuiper Belt sculpted.","color":"#d5bcfe"}},{"id":"60ae607a-3f55-48cc-81e0-fe9a4ea03602","position":{"x":750,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Present","summary":"Stable orbits for ~3.8 Gyr; the Sun is mid-life, with ~5 Gyr remaining before red-giant phase.","color":"#d5bcfe"}}],"connections":[{"id":"f0180a43-9ada-4a1e-ae05-a39601e6c48a","from":"7bcec2f8-3e62-4c54-ade0-770a4f7680aa","to":"463b7b16-3123-49da-939b-466a6e6e9d02","data":{"label":"collapsed into","style":"solid"}},{"id":"68fd1451-f99a-493e-988b-51bd94f6f5d8","from":"463b7b16-3123-49da-939b-466a6e6e9d02","to":"d85476d4-de2f-4304-87cb-b626d95c55e3","data":{"label":"seeded","style":"solid"}},{"id":"ef4a6f77-e73c-4d48-afdb-1f34171eeadb","from":"d85476d4-de2f-4304-87cb-b626d95c55e3","to":"dae92517-52ff-442d-bfb9-817b427ebb96","data":{"label":"accreted into","style":"solid"}},{"id":"5a660997-3a53-4f07-a052-75842c2d536e","from":"dae92517-52ff-442d-bfb9-817b427ebb96","to":"0a2aa224-8d3c-4cfb-8a5f-1dd3f2904783","data":{"label":"preceded","style":"solid"}},{"id":"7ee21e13-4d0b-40c5-8234-9e12fb9239ef","from":"0a2aa224-8d3c-4cfb-8a5f-1dd3f2904783","to":"9da0937b-342d-4ff6-a5a0-a53d46efec2d","data":{"label":"preceded","style":"solid"}},{"id":"58e7de77-7832-41f9-bd80-8a4baa456dce","from":"9da0937b-342d-4ff6-a5a0-a53d46efec2d","to":"60ae607a-3f55-48cc-81e0-fe9a4ea03602","data":{"label":"settled into","style":"solid"}},{"id":"a36f12b2-f8f4-4740-a522-706f5d5291e4","from":"463b7b16-3123-49da-939b-466a6e6e9d02","to":"0a2aa224-8d3c-4cfb-8a5f-1dd3f2904783","data":{"label":"shaped","style":"dashed"}}],"containers":[{"id":"2141be14-48f7-4c04-84ed-6a42ef2eb359","nodes":["7bcec2f8-3e62-4c54-ade0-770a4f7680aa","463b7b16-3123-49da-939b-466a6e6e9d02","d85476d4-de2f-4304-87cb-b626d95c55e3"],"data":{"label":"Early system","formation":"linear-horizontal","style":"dashed"}},{"id":"638e0cd1-232c-4f32-bd4b-486cb2bd81a4","nodes":["0a2aa224-8d3c-4cfb-8a5f-1dd3f2904783","9da0937b-342d-4ff6-a5a0-a53d46efec2d","60ae607a-3f55-48cc-81e0-fe9a4ea03602"],"data":{"label":"Stabilization","formation":"linear-horizontal","style":"none"}}],"parentLayerId":"83326a2b-3774-4957-8878-e1280bab010f","parentNodeId":"da351a86-2756-49f4-aff2-7625f7fc0d73"}
{"type":"layer","id":"05e22a74-732b-4c05-b428-f22d613ff0b9","level":3,"layout":"linear-vertical","nodes":[{"id":"2be3b5a3-ca44-4785-931d-a283e7c62b70","position":{"x":0,"y":-300},"data":{"label":"Crust","summary":"0–70 km deep; rigid silicate rock; oceanic basalt or continental granite; carries the biosphere.","color":"#ffb8cc"}},{"id":"12100c56-cda1-4332-af46-5f99021fb9e1","position":{"x":0,"y":-100},"data":{"label":"Mantle","summary":"70–2890 km; hot silicate rock that creeps over millions of years; convection drives plate tectonics.","color":"#ffb8cc"}},{"id":"e3eb2565-f4ef-445d-b9ff-d0cd1e5c4c4e","position":{"x":0,"y":100},"data":{"label":"Outer Core","summary":"2890–5150 km; liquid iron-nickel at ~4500 °C; its convection generates Earth's magnetic field.","color":"#ffb8cc"}},{"id":"30e0fb7c-e482-4207-93b6-1b859b3815e4","position":{"x":0,"y":300},"data":{"label":"Inner Core","summary":"5150–6371 km; solid iron-nickel ball at ~5200 °C; rotates slightly faster than the surface.","color":"#fee7c3"}}],"connections":[{"id":"b9f9a069-6d1f-4c36-9b3b-5e6708ae9d74","from":"2be3b5a3-ca44-4785-931d-a283e7c62b70","to":"12100c56-cda1-4332-af46-5f99021fb9e1","data":{"label":"overlies","style":"solid"}},{"id":"fc49552f-9a78-464a-919a-1c54b1352b5d","from":"12100c56-cda1-4332-af46-5f99021fb9e1","to":"e3eb2565-f4ef-445d-b9ff-d0cd1e5c4c4e","data":{"label":"overlies","style":"solid"}},{"id":"a8038077-0915-4ca8-8bd1-8a20d6e571ea","from":"e3eb2565-f4ef-445d-b9ff-d0cd1e5c4c4e","to":"30e0fb7c-e482-4207-93b6-1b859b3815e4","data":{"label":"overlies","style":"solid"}},{"id":"8431bd5c-b467-4d61-bb8f-c0bce72c3704","from":"e3eb2565-f4ef-445d-b9ff-d0cd1e5c4c4e","to":"2be3b5a3-ca44-4785-931d-a283e7c62b70","data":{"label":"generates magnetic field at","style":"dashed"}}],"containers":[{"id":"58899875-685e-4062-86a3-e9e116f42981","nodes":["12100c56-cda1-4332-af46-5f99021fb9e1","e3eb2565-f4ef-445d-b9ff-d0cd1e5c4c4e"],"data":{"label":"Convecting layers","formation":"linear-vertical","style":"dashed"}}],"parentLayerId":"52452a6c-85fb-479a-adef-ac83b498c4ec","parentNodeId":"f7e89a19-3437-4911-ae52-9cdbae72b8df"}
{"type":"layer","id":"61750e72-6586-4565-bbd3-f701c2910e09","level":3,"layout":"radial","nodes":[{"id":"d4e8fdcc-32bc-4205-a46b-553c97bbfab2","position":{"x":0,"y":-280},"data":{"label":"Great Red Spot","summary":"An anticyclonic storm 16 000 km wide; observed continuously since 1830; rotates once every 6 Earth-days.","color":"#ffbdfb"}},{"id":"40921a6d-db74-4643-980c-b99c74cb8542","position":{"x":266,"y":-87},"data":{"label":"Cloud Bands","summary":"Alternating light zones (rising NH₃ ice) and dark belts (descending warmer gas); 100 m/s zonal jets between.","color":"#ffbdfb"}},{"id":"1ce01944-0844-4921-8a43-3acbfa55558d","position":{"x":165,"y":227},"data":{"label":"95 Known Moons","summary":"Includes the four Galilean moons (Io, Europa, Ganymede, Callisto); Ganymede is larger than Mercury.","color":"#ffbdfb"}},{"id":"ab158441-1c29-4f5f-b3c8-0858f1b971a4","position":{"x":-165,"y":227},"data":{"label":"Strong Magnetic Field","summary":"20 000× Earth's at the source; magnetosphere reaches past Saturn's orbit; powers intense aurorae.","color":"#ffbdfb"}},{"id":"401c9fae-2131-4bef-876e-dcb56f033ad4","position":{"x":-266,"y":-87},"data":{"label":"Ring System","summary":"Thin dusty rings discovered by Voyager 1 in 1979; sourced by micrometeoroid impacts on small inner moons.","color":"#ffbdfb"}}],"connections":[{"id":"8019edde-4c0f-4027-b95d-28df722bd42f","from":"ab158441-1c29-4f5f-b3c8-0858f1b971a4","to":"1ce01944-0844-4921-8a43-3acbfa55558d","data":{"label":"shields","style":"dashed"}}],"containers":[],"parentLayerId":"5995a5ac-95b9-4366-a6f3-4ad757618139","parentNodeId":"bca1da80-1152-4410-9629-3041f9c4ee65"}
{"type":"layer","id":"3ac77c29-80cd-4b2b-a775-7b352e13e6b7","level":3,"layout":"linear-horizontal","nodes":[{"id":"ea3835bf-dd90-4611-b740-cdd36cc95f54","position":{"x":-420,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Trigger ~4.1 Gya","summary":"Outer planets cross orbital resonances; gravity perturbs the early Kuiper Belt, flinging bodies inward.","color":"#d2c09a"}},{"id":"327d894d-1747-422a-a6b5-2fcac1dccf6d","position":{"x":-140,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Peak ~3.9 Gya","summary":"Impact flux on the Moon ~1000× present-day rate; basins like Imbrium (1100 km) and Orientale (930 km) form.","color":"#d2c09a"}},{"id":"1890186d-71a4-4f1b-a311-b554c838e8ca","position":{"x":140,"y":0},"data":{"label":"Lunar Mare Formation","summary":"Impact basins flood with basaltic lava 3.9–3.0 Gya, creating the dark mare visible from Earth today.","color":"#d2c09a"}},{"id":"6595225c-d4a2-4964-a231-82d8bf17c9bd","position":{"x":420,"y":0},"data":{"label":"End ~3.8 Gya","summary":"Bombardment rate drops as the inner-solar-system population is depleted; relatively quiet era begins.","color":"#d2c09a"}}],"connections":[{"id":"d19912bd-68a5-4fc4-95c7-48dc21dd7065","from":"ea3835bf-dd90-4611-b740-cdd36cc95f54","to":"327d894d-1747-422a-a6b5-2fcac1dccf6d","data":{"label":"preceded","style":"solid"}},{"id":"9cc93712-bae1-477c-8011-51ee3882ddfb","from":"327d894d-1747-422a-a6b5-2fcac1dccf6d","to":"1890186d-71a4-4f1b-a311-b554c838e8ca","data":{"label":"triggered","style":"solid"}},{"id":"16ba0099-1040-4dc3-9484-925c7accc496","from":"1890186d-71a4-4f1b-a311-b554c838e8ca","to":"6595225c-d4a2-4964-a231-82d8bf17c9bd","data":{"label":"preceded","style":"solid"}}],"containers":[],"parentLayerId":"24e2c8f9-1cd7-477d-bedc-9c7ae9fd0511","parentNodeId":"0a2aa224-8d3c-4cfb-8a5f-1dd3f2904783"}